      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\), we need to determine the values of \\(x\\) for which the expression is non-negative. We start by analyzing the numerator and the denominator separately.\n\nFirst, consider the numerator \\(x + 6\\):\n\\[\nx + 6 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq -6\n\\]\nThis means that \\(x\\) cannot be \\(-6\\).\n\nNext, consider the denominator \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\):\n\\[\nx^2 + 2x + 7\n\\]\nWe need to determine if the denominator can be zero or negative. The quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\) because the discriminant of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + 2x + 7 = 0\\) is:\n\\[\n\\Delta = 2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 7 = 4 - 28 = -24\n\\]\nSince the discriminant is negative, the quadratic equation has no real roots, and the quadratic expression \\(x^2 + 2x + 7\\) is always positive for all real \\(x\\).\n\nTherefore, the only restriction on \\(x\\) is \\(x \\neq -6\\). Since the denominator is always positive, the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) simplifies to finding where the numerator \\(x + 6\\) is non-negative.\n\nThe expression \\(x + 6\\) is non-negative when:\n\\[\nx + 6 \\ge 0 \\implies x \\ge -6\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the inequality \\(\\frac{x + 6}{x^2 + 2x + 7} \\ge 0\\) is:\n\\[\nx \\in [-6, \\infty)\n\\]\n\nPutting the final answer in interval notation, we have:\n\\[\n\\boxed{[-6, \\infty)}\n\\]",
